 addUserKnob {20 howto l "How to use"}
 addUserKnob {26 howto_h l "" +STARTLINE T "<b>dhPerspGuide marks one direction in the scene</b>"}
 addUserKnob {26 howto_s l "" +STARTLINE T "<b>1.</b> Put this under your plate.<br><b>2.</b> Find two edges that are parallel in real life but converge on screen. Drag <i>line 1</i> onto one and <i>line 2</i> onto the other, stretching each as long as the edge allows. They do not have to be on the ground.<br><b>3.</b> Set <i>these lines are</i>: <b>ground</b> for a direction running away from you, <b>across</b> for one running left to right in front of you, <b>vertical</b> for upright edges.<br><b>4.</b> Add a second guide for the other level direction, at right angles to the first. Add a third on the uprights if you like; it makes the solve better and it is recognized on its own.<br><b>5.</b> Drop a <b>dhPerspSolve</b> under the last one."}
 addUserKnob {26 howto_d l "" +STARTLINE}
 addUserKnob {26 howto_n l "" +STARTLINE T "<b>Picking edges.</b> The two lines must follow the same real direction. Two curbs on opposite sides of a street, the top and bottom of the same wall, two window sills on the same facade. Two edges that only look parallel will give a confident wrong answer.<br><br><b>Longer is better, and by a lot.</b> Place each end within about a pixel and the line's angle is uncertain by roughly 2/L radians for a line L pixels long, and the vanishing point error follows it straight through. A 200 pixel line carries about 0.6 degrees of uncertainty, an 800 pixel line about 0.14: four times longer is four times better. Trace the longest clean run of the edge you can find. It makes no difference where along the edge the two points sit, only how far apart they are.<br><br><b>Lens distortion, if you ever need it.</b> A lens bends straight edges a little, most in the corners of frame and most on a wide one, and this fits a straight line to whatever you trace. Usually too small to be worth a thought. On a wide plate that will not settle, trace nearer the middle of frame, and if that is not enough put a LensDistortion above the guides.<br><br><b>Height does not matter.</b> Every level edge running the same way shares a vanishing point whatever height it is at, so a roofline works as well as a curb and is usually easier to trace.<br><br><b>More than two lines.</b> <i>add line</i> on the PerspLines tab draws extra lines out through the same vanishing point, for checking the fit against other edges in the plate. They do not change the solve."}
